00:03:01.478 --> 00:03:06.992
And if each of you could share perhaps
your favorite academic experience,

00:03:06.992 --> 00:03:09.714
so maybe a class project, faculty.

00:03:09.714 --> 00:03:14.668
What was your favorite academic
experience when you were at the GSB?

00:03:14.668 --> 00:03:16.453
And we'll have each of you answer that and

00:03:16.453 --> 00:03:18.454
then we'll get started
with more questions.

00:03:18.454 --> 00:03:20.223
So Norris, why don't we start with you?

00:03:20.223 --> 00:03:22.672
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks for the introduction.

00:03:22.672 --> 00:03:24.887
So, again, my name is Norris.

00:03:24.887 --> 00:03:27.537
And I graduated class of 2019.

00:03:27.537 --> 00:03:31.789
And one academic program I
really liked was the SSEP, or

00:03:31.789 --> 00:03:36.689
Stafford Supply Exchange Program I
did in the fall of 2018.

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.754
And that was really cool
because I got to learn about

00:03:40.754 --> 00:03:45.076
on ByteDance before it became
super huge in the United States.

00:03:45.076 --> 00:03:46.719
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:49.984
Sunny, why don't we go next with you?

00:03:49.984 --> 00:03:51.367
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone.

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:52.725
My name is Sunny Kumar.

00:03:52.725 --> 00:03:57.561
I'm a graduate of the MBA program as
well as the MD program from Stanford.

00:03:57.561 --> 00:04:01.394
My favorite academic
experience was leading a GST,

00:04:01.394 --> 00:04:06.359
a global study trip, to Greece,
where we looked at how the economic

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:10.647
crisis from 2009 affected
their healthcare system.

00:04:10.647 --> 00:04:14.312
Even more relevant today, as we're all
facing another healthcare crisis and

00:04:14.312 --> 00:04:16.268
learning how to adapt in this situation.

00:04:16.268 --> 00:04:18.392
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:04:18.392 --> 00:04:22.864
Marlo, I know you're on by phone and
we'd love to have you introduce yourself.

00:04:25.622 --> 00:04:26.972
&gt;&gt; Excellent, hi everyone.

00:04:26.972 --> 00:04:30.565
This is Marlo, apologies again
that I don't have video, but

00:04:30.565 --> 00:04:33.201
quick background on me, class of 2016.

00:04:33.201 --> 00:04:37.275
I currently work in private
equity now in Los Angeles.

00:04:37.275 --> 00:04:41.785
And I would my favorite academic
experience was actually probably taking

00:04:41.785 --> 00:04:46.295
the search fun class and learning
a little bit about almost a micro private

00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:49.586
equity type world combined
with entrepreneurship.

00:04:49.586 --> 00:04:52.084
I think it was a really interesting
way to learn about business.

00:04:52.084 --> 00:04:52.821
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:04:52.821 --> 00:04:54.708
Mariana, why don't you go next?

00:04:54.708 --> 00:04:58.989
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hey everyone,
I'm Mariana, from 2013 class.

00:04:58.989 --> 00:05:02.871
I work at Facebook now
as a product manager.

00:05:02.871 --> 00:05:08.138
And my favorite academic experience, For

00:05:08.138 --> 00:05:11.788
me, it was the electronic
business class with Mendelson.

00:05:11.788 --> 00:05:15.306
I know it seems it sounds so basic,
but I come from the East Coast.

00:05:15.306 --> 00:05:17.616
And I didn't really
know enough about tech.

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.295
And it really opened my eyes.

00:05:19.295 --> 00:05:23.860
And I remember our final exam was about
Airbnb and I was like, this is crazy.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.288
And now I'm like, my gosh,
it's the biggest business ever.

00:05:27.288 --> 00:05:30.021
[LAUGH] So it was really great exposure.

00:05:30.021 --> 00:05:31.382
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:05:31.382 --> 00:05:32.542
Thanks for sharing.

00:05:32.542 --> 00:05:34.620
And Vidya,
we'll have you introduce yourself now.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.518
&gt;&gt; Everyone, I'm Vidya.

00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:39.655
I graduated from the GSB in 2012.

00:05:39.655 --> 00:05:41.836
And now I am up in Seattle working for

00:05:41.836 --> 00:05:45.343
the Strategic Investment Fund
at the Gates Foundation.

00:05:45.343 --> 00:05:50.447
My favorite academic experience at the GSB
was the bio design innovation class,

00:05:50.447 --> 00:05:53.911
which I hope they're still teaching,
I'm not sure.

00:05:53.911 --> 00:05:57.755
Which is kind of an interdisciplinary
class between the business school,

00:05:57.755 --> 00:06:00.813
medical school, and
a bunch of the engineering programs.

00:06:00.813 --> 00:06:05.868
Where you basically have a specific
medical need that you choose and

00:06:05.868 --> 00:06:08.891
you develop a medical device together.

00:06:08.891 --> 00:06:12.468
Kind of go through the entire process of,
kind of the design process

00:06:12.468 --> 00:06:15.940
to come up with something and
figure out the regulatory pathway.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.570
And my team, the year that I did it,
they were focused on global health needs.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:24.718
And I got to basically develop
a saliva-based diagnostic for

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:26.504
neonatal jaundice.

00:06:26.504 --> 00:06:27.766
It was just such a cool experience and

00:06:27.766 --> 00:06:30.101
working across people with several
backgrounds, so I loved that.

00:06:32.457 --> 00:06:34.076
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you so much for sharing.

00:06:34.076 --> 00:06:38.959
I see actually a question that's a great
follow up to you guys sharing your

00:06:38.959 --> 00:06:41.183
favorite academic experience.

00:06:41.183 --> 00:06:45.017
We got asked really about
the leadership courses and

00:06:45.017 --> 00:06:47.638
leadership curriculum at the GSB.

00:06:47.638 --> 00:06:51.580
I was wondering if maybe one or
two of you could share,

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.539
of the leadership courses that you maybe
took, whether it was leadership labs or

00:06:57.539 --> 00:07:01.306
an elective that you took or
executive challenge,

00:07:01.306 --> 00:07:06.851
this is a question from Andrew Hue,
what was most transformative for you?

00:07:06.851 --> 00:07:10.393
And really, how did it help you
develop your leadership skills?

00:07:10.393 --> 00:07:13.138
So kind of a follow up academic question.

00:07:13.138 --> 00:07:17.372
Why don't we start with
Norris to answer that?

00:07:19.902 --> 00:07:23.152
&gt;&gt; Sure, so one thing about leadership
labs that I've taken with me,

00:07:23.152 --> 00:07:26.472
it's pretty basic, it's like
the action impact feedback format.

00:07:26.472 --> 00:07:28.012
And in our startup,

00:07:28.012 --> 00:07:32.910
we have monthly climate checks
where we actually utilize that.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.989
And it seems to be a pretty good way
where you get specific information about

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:39.463
something you did and
how it impacts others.

00:07:39.463 --> 00:07:43.639
Pretty simple, but [LAUGH] often people
forget that they don't actually know what

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:45.407
you did to make them feel that way.

00:07:45.407 --> 00:07:46.927
So it's good to remember that.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:50.580
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks for sharing.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.540
Sunny, could you share about leadership,

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:58.180
perhaps something that helped you
develop your leadership skills or

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:02.830
helped kind of on your
transformation through business law?

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:03.884
Absolutely, for

00:08:03.884 --> 00:08:08.241
me the most impactful academic experience
I had related to leadership was

00:08:08.241 --> 00:08:12.893
a course called interpersonal dynamics
that many of you may be familiar with.

00:08:12.893 --> 00:08:15.828
The core nature of this
course is to help you,

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:20.000
in a small group, learn how to
communicate more effectively and

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.806
in a different style than I
think most people are aware of.

00:08:23.806 --> 00:08:25.526
Coming from a medical background,

00:08:25.526 --> 00:08:29.495
we often are in positions where we're
delivering hard news, challenging news.

00:08:29.495 --> 00:08:34.911
But I actually think unequivocally the
best communication experience and learning

00:08:34.911 --> 00:08:39.800
I got was from interpersonal dynamics
during my entire tenure at Stanford.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.322
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:08:42.322 --> 00:08:46.398
I see another question out there
just about career pivoting.

00:08:46.398 --> 00:08:49.470
And that one we shared this screen for
a few of you,

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.810
there were some of you who
did pivot in your careers.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:58.381
I'd love to hear from, we'll start with
Mariana about what you were doing before

00:08:58.381 --> 00:09:02.905
the GSB, and maybe how the GSB helped you
prepare for what you wanted to do after.

00:09:02.905 --> 00:09:06.183
And then I'd also love for
Marlo to follow up and answer that too.

00:09:06.183 --> 00:09:08.897
But we'll start with Mariana.

00:09:08.897 --> 00:09:11.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
career pivoting is very common.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:15.673
Before business school,
I was in consulting and private equity.

00:09:15.673 --> 00:09:18.960
And I thought I was going to go
back to private equity afterwards.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.750
That's what I was gearing myself towards.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:23.235
I actually ended up switching into tech.

00:09:23.235 --> 00:09:25.070
So now I work at Facebook.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.130
And a lot of that just came from exposure.

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.659
I think that's one of the best
things about Stanford is that

00:09:31.659 --> 00:09:33.784
the alumni come back, obviously.

00:09:33.784 --> 00:09:36.918
[LAUGH] And in courses,
you hear from a lot of

00:09:36.918 --> 00:09:41.870
folks who are running major
companies in different industries.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.732
So it gave me a lot more perspective.

00:09:43.732 --> 00:09:46.452
So being on the east coast,
for me, finance and

00:09:46.452 --> 00:09:49.999
consulting were the biggest and
most important industries.

00:09:49.999 --> 00:09:51.287
And then I come to the west coast and

00:09:51.287 --> 00:09:54.410
I realized actually there's
a whole other world out there.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:59.890
And so for me, was gaining exposure
to the industry of tech and

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:04.090
then narrowing once I figured
out that's where I wanted to go.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:09.300
Things that helped were coursework just
to get the survey of all the things, and

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:13.100
then the internship is basically
like a free pass to do anything.

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:18.520
So I contacted this founder who I know,
asked to be his number two,

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:21.810
and basically just followed him
around the world for my summer.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:26.598
And that's how I figured out it was
definitely the industry for me.

00:10:26.598 --> 00:10:29.530
And so I think it's pretty common.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.580
I'd say, keep an open mind, and don't be
surprised if you go in thinking one thing

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.080
and then end up doing something
entirely different afterwards.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:38.525
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:10:38.525 --> 00:10:39.730
Marlo, do you want to answer that too?

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.220
I know you pivoted when you
came to business school too.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:46.015
&gt;&gt; Sure, so before business school,

00:10:46.015 --> 00:10:50.789
I was in consulting on a kind of very
traditional path to return to consulting.

00:10:50.789 --> 00:10:54.758
Then got to the GSB, and similarly,
I got exposed to a bunch of new people,

00:10:54.758 --> 00:10:59.182
a bunch of new ideas, and realized I was
actually really interested in investing.

00:10:59.182 --> 00:11:03.184
And so I was pretty fortunate that
I realized that fairly early and

00:11:03.184 --> 00:11:07.332
ultimately worked with the Career Center
to pursue an internship in

00:11:07.332 --> 00:11:09.600
private equity on the East Coast.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:13.230
Had a great experience,
realized it was what I wanted to

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:17.685
do kind of full time after the GSB, and so
ultimately decided to go down that path.

00:11:17.685 --> 00:11:20.388
And so I've been in Los Angeles
doing private equity for

00:11:20.388 --> 00:11:24.429
the last four years, kind of building upon
some of what I learned in my internship.

00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:27.490
And really glad that I got the chance
over that three-month period to

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:28.774
try out a different career.

00:11:28.774 --> 00:11:31.558
Because I think otherwise,
as much as I really enjoyed consulting,

00:11:31.558 --> 00:11:33.390
I think it's been exciting
to make a change.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:35.790
&gt;&gt; Great, thanks, you guys.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:40.010
There's actually a question
from Anna in the audience.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:42.810
She's kind of piggybacking off of
this career pivoting question.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.955
So I'll follow up with Mariana and
Marlo but

00:11:46.955 --> 00:11:50.998
then probably have someone else chime in.

00:11:50.998 --> 00:11:55.138
That really is about, how many of you came
to business school with the intent to

00:11:55.138 --> 00:11:58.602
pivot, and how many of you were
exposed to something while here?

00:11:58.602 --> 00:12:03.065
And so many of you are doing such
different things out there that I'd love

00:12:03.065 --> 00:12:05.715
to hear, a follow up to Mariana and Marlo.

00:12:05.715 --> 00:12:09.612
But then maybe also, Bidya,
you could chime in here too.

00:12:09.612 --> 00:12:14.390
So Mariana, it sounded like you
were exposed to things as you came.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:16.900
So maybe you didn't have
the intent of pivoting, but

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:21.304
if you could share more of what you were
thinking as you came into business school.

00:12:21.304 --> 00:12:26.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I did not intend to pivot, so
I was in private equity, and I expected

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:32.090
to remain in private equity but just in a
different geography and a different scale.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:36.504
And when I got to business
school I basically

00:12:36.504 --> 00:12:40.211
figured out that I wanted to create.

00:12:40.211 --> 00:12:45.278
And for me, I got value from like
actually creating things that without me,

00:12:45.278 --> 00:12:50.281
in the world wouldn't exist, and
I wasn't getting that from investing.

00:12:50.281 --> 00:12:53.970
And so it just became really clear
where I was getting my energy from.

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.986
And I think it's been a good
barometer of my life is,

00:12:56.986 --> 00:12:59.882
what drains your energy,
what gives you energy?

00:12:59.882 --> 00:13:04.020
And so you can try on a few things and the
way to try things on in business school is

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:06.910
through coursework, through your peers.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:13.140
So almost everyone has done a career
that could be interesting for you.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.247
And so just by talking to folks in
your class, you can get a sense for

00:13:16.247 --> 00:13:18.591
what it's really like and
ask honest questions.

00:13:18.591 --> 00:13:21.677
Because no one has a vested
interest in lying or

00:13:21.677 --> 00:13:24.810
persuading you one way or another.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:25.780
You're there with them for

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:28.940
two years, most people aren't trying
to push you one way or another.

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.540
So it's a really good safe
space to get that background.

00:13:32.540 --> 00:13:39.000
And then going into sort
of what I did after school,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:45.880
I started my own company, which gave me
a lot of different functions within tech.

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:50.330
And then when I switched into Facebook,
I narrowed even further into product.

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.180
So not a straight line, but

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:56.690
Stanford helped me figure out
what's the general playing field.

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.305
Okay, where within that I want to play?

00:13:58.305 --> 00:13:59.441
Okay, why?

00:13:59.441 --> 00:14:01.550
And then the launchpad to pursue it.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:03.605
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:14:03.605 --> 00:14:07.470
Marlo, maybe you could chime in,
and then we'll go to Bidya.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:12.800
&gt;&gt; Perfect, yeah, I would say mine
was somewhat exposure based as well.

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:17.520
I mean, I had a combination of kind of
meeting classmates and putting faces

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.510
to the career with respect to something
I'd heard of, but didn't know quite as

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:24.910
much about until I met individuals who
were in that path or come from that path.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.220
I also took a couple courses,
I took an investing class,

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:31.320
a couple investing classes,
I took some financial modeling classes.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:34.371
I also joined some clubs,
I was in a couple investing clubs and

00:14:34.371 --> 00:14:36.085
private equity club and whatnot.

00:14:36.085 --> 00:14:40.387
And I think that helped also just sort of
surround me with new ideas that made me

00:14:40.387 --> 00:14:43.108
realize that was something
I was interested in.

00:14:43.108 --> 00:14:46.936
&gt;&gt; Excellent, and Bidya, I know you
also switched careers and pivoted.

00:14:46.936 --> 00:14:48.161
When you came,

00:14:48.161 --> 00:14:53.445
was that kind of a goal of yours to
pivot when you started at the GSB?

00:14:53.445 --> 00:14:56.820
&gt;&gt; I think for
me there was definitely intent to pivot.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:15:01.750
So my background before coming to the GSB
is I worked in kind of traditional finance

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:05.260
in New York at Goldman, first starting
on the investment banking side,

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.430
then working within the kind
of prop hedge fund.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:11.490
And I knew when I was doing that,
that I was gaining skills, but

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:16.180
I wanted to apply those skills somewhere
very different in the course of my career.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:19.070
But found that when you work
that many hours a day or

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.420
a week, you can't really spend a lot
of time thinking about the transition.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:27.443
And for me, my passion area had always
been around international development.

00:15:27.443 --> 00:15:29.383
And I think through actually
my time at Goldman,

00:15:29.383 --> 00:15:31.050
I gained a lot of interest in healthcare.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.480
So I became interested in global health.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:37.754
But the problem that I faced was there's
a bazillion things you can go do in global

00:15:37.754 --> 00:15:38.258
health.

00:15:38.258 --> 00:15:41.170
And I have this particular set of
skills that relates to kind of

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:44.030
understanding companies and
how the private sector works.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:47.350
And so how do I figure out what
that right role is for me?

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:50.330
So I came into business school
with the intent to explore that.

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:55.109
So not knowing what the answer would be,
but wanting to look at, maybe working for

00:15:55.109 --> 00:15:57.687
the government or multilateral agencies.

00:15:57.687 --> 00:15:59.798
Maybe working for
companies on the ground or for

00:15:59.798 --> 00:16:03.230
US companies that were kind of trying
to send innovation over there.

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:06.970
And maybe staying in the investment field,
which is a function I really liked.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:11.180
But having that investment be much more
kind of geared towards bringing innovation

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:12.840
to the developing world.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.165
And I did my internship at
the Gates Foundation, which I feel so

00:16:16.165 --> 00:16:18.067
grateful to have been at Stanford.

00:16:18.067 --> 00:16:18.892
because that year,

00:16:18.892 --> 00:16:23.340
when 2011, my summer was the first
year they offered an internship.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:28.360
And the only places they offered it
were Stanford Business School and Udub.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.000
Just because actually,

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.854
the CEO of the Gates Foundation at
the time had was a Stanford alum.

00:16:32.854 --> 00:16:33.920
That was the only reason.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:35.860
It was very fortuitous.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:39.922
I actually did my internship totally out
of investing, focused on global health,

00:16:39.922 --> 00:16:42.893
got a lot of exposure, and
found this team that I now work on.

00:16:42.893 --> 00:16:46.323
Which was kind of the perfect combination
of the skills and the interests, and

00:16:46.323 --> 00:16:47.230
found my way there.

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:51.080
But I also did a lot of searching,
like the others said, in classes and

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:55.610
projects, and demixes, which are the small
internships you can do over the summer.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:56.430
I worked in India.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:02.415
So it was just a great kind of test
ground, I think, to try different things.

00:17:02.415 --> 00:17:04.431
&gt;&gt; Great, yeah, for sure.

00:17:04.431 --> 00:17:09.434
I'd love for Sunny to chime in
here about intend to pivot,

00:17:09.434 --> 00:17:14.040
but I'm going to broaden it a bit for
you specifically.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:19.413
We have a few questions in
the audience about dual degrees,

00:17:19.413 --> 00:17:24.796
and I'm coming to the GSB, and
kind of how is that possible?

00:17:24.796 --> 00:17:28.937
So I'd love for
you to share when you decided doing

00:17:28.937 --> 00:17:32.399
a dual degree was the right thing for you?

00:17:32.399 --> 00:17:36.042
And just what your intent, or
original thinking was at that time, and

00:17:36.042 --> 00:17:38.440
maybe how it laid out as
you went to business.

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:39.320
&gt;&gt; Sure, absolutely.

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:43.164
I'll give you guys a little bit of
context in that when I came to Stanford,

00:17:43.164 --> 00:17:47.194
I knew that the entire university was well
known for having a lot of support for

00:17:47.194 --> 00:17:49.520
dual degrees at the Medical School.

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:53.050
Almost every student does not graduate
in the traditional four years.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:55.880
They take at least one
year to do something else.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.260
And when it came time for
me to make that decision,

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.570
I did not actually know that it
was going to be business school.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:01.656
I wanted to explore.

00:18:01.656 --> 00:18:05.500
And the primary thing I was motivated
by was I wanted to expand my horizons.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:07.294
I knew medicine very well.

00:18:07.294 --> 00:18:08.810
I knew clinical research very well.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:11.090
Many of my colleagues continued
to double down on that.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:12.390
I knew I wanted to do something else.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:16.515
So when I was in clinical rotations, I
found that there's just such a big divide

00:18:16.515 --> 00:18:19.738
between how physicians think
medicine should be practiced.

00:18:19.738 --> 00:18:24.602
And how healthcare administrators
actually implement medical practice,

00:18:24.602 --> 00:18:27.760
and I wanted to get to
the bottom of that gap.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:31.400
And I thought the best way to do so would
be to get a business school education.

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:34.180
Build out that experience,
return to clinical practice, and

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.370
then be able to kind of navigate
both sides of that course.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:40.589
I think as Mariana said,
it was not a straight line path for me.

00:18:40.589 --> 00:18:42.723
There were many twists and
curves along the way.

00:18:42.723 --> 00:18:46.753
But perhaps the most impactful thing
about doing a dual degree at Stanford

00:18:46.753 --> 00:18:49.418
was that it just gave me
such a broad exposure to so

00:18:49.418 --> 00:18:54.170
many things outside of my specific
specialty, which was clinical medicine.

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:55.061
And as a result,

00:18:55.061 --> 00:18:59.332
I had the opportunity to intern in venture
capital to found a business of my own,

00:18:59.332 --> 00:19:03.360
to work on a number of different
actually global health projects.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.081
And at the end of that, for me,

00:19:05.081 --> 00:19:09.865
the biggest thing was where can I have the
biggest impact, the most outsized impact?

00:19:09.865 --> 00:19:12.574
And at least early in my career,
I thought the best way for

00:19:12.574 --> 00:19:15.280
me to do that would be to
go into venture capital.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:20.167
And have the opportunity to fund
healthcare technology startups that can

00:19:20.167 --> 00:19:25.526
bring tech that's much needed into
healthcare into the ecosystem much faster,

00:19:25.526 --> 00:19:27.437
and much more efficiently.

00:19:27.437 --> 00:19:30.010
&gt;&gt; Excellent, thank you so much.

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:35.957
There was a question in the audience
specifically around entrepreneurship,

00:19:35.957 --> 00:19:39.388
and starting ventures
from being at the GSB.

00:19:39.388 --> 00:19:44.360
And Norris, I'd love for you to share
a bit more about your journey so

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:49.954
far from kind of aerospace engineering,
pre-GSB, to starting a company.

00:19:49.954 --> 00:19:55.173
How did the GSB specifically equip you,
and help you along that journey?

00:19:55.173 --> 00:19:59.570
And why did you decide to go to
business school given that goal?

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:02.480
&gt;&gt; Cool.
Yeah, so for me,

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.130
it's been a pretty long journey to
start the company that I have now,

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:08.910
which is sort of the supersonic
commercial aviation company.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.353
Well, I had the idea when
I was in high school.

00:20:15.353 --> 00:20:17.670
It was just generally about faster flight.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:21.840
I even wrote my admissions essay or
GSB about it.

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:27.737
And so a lot of like career path,
including my study led to that.

00:20:27.737 --> 00:20:30.562
And so going to the GSB was
really the final step for

00:20:30.562 --> 00:20:32.453
me to really start this company.

00:20:32.453 --> 00:20:35.921
Because I gained a lot of industry
experience that I knew what

00:20:35.921 --> 00:20:41.110
the solution was to future travel, and
they just needed to start a company.

00:20:41.110 --> 00:20:44.166
And so I went to GSB because
of a number of factors.

00:20:44.166 --> 00:20:47.661
One, kind of like what Sunny said,
it has a very strong departments across

00:20:47.661 --> 00:20:50.350
the entire campus,
not only business school.

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:54.271
In his case, medicine, in my case,
the aerospace engineering department.

00:20:54.271 --> 00:20:58.620
And that's where I went to actually find
my co-founder, who I work with today.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.250
So [COUGH] that's that's one element.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:05.440
Secondly, of course, the GSB is
really great, because of the classes.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:12.160
The professors, the students, and
all of them helps in some way or another.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:17.080
I could be more specific if you want,
in helping me.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:20.090
And in addition to that,
the location is really great.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:24.980
I could literally bike to several VC
offices between classes, after class,

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:27.175
or on the weekends, or whatever.

00:21:27.175 --> 00:21:31.221
And that made it very convenient to
actually go talk to a lot of VCs,

00:21:31.221 --> 00:21:33.840
establish that relationship with them.

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.004
There are some sacrifices, of course, with
starting a company during business school.

00:21:38.004 --> 00:21:40.232
I spent a lot of time building a business,

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:44.260
which I basically started in
the first two weeks of school.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.871
So I didn't really do classes
as well as I could have.

00:21:46.871 --> 00:21:48.390
And my social life [LAUGH],

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:52.960
I had to prioritize my significant other
over my social life, in that regard.

00:21:54.828 --> 00:21:59.280
&gt;&gt; Excellent, and Norris, could you share
one of the more specific ways in which you

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:05.850
really felt supported, or kind of
transformed on your journey is something

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:10.470
that really helped you accomplish
your goals with starting a company?

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:11.899
You mentioned that you
could be more specific.

00:22:11.899 --> 00:22:13.070
Was it a class?

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.060
Was it a club activity?

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:16.480
Was it something that you did?

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:22.320
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's really hard to pinpoint
just one thing, because, I mean,

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:27.368
there's tens of things at GSP that really
helped me build the company, right?

00:22:27.368 --> 00:22:29.870
I mean, one was just finding my
co-founder, which is, of course,

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:31.950
a very monumental part of it.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.660
And I simply just did that by emailing
some students over there, and

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:37.830
got in touch with the right person.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.010
But one thing I'll mention
is a professor on campus,

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:45.860
and he was a board member of an airline.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.802
And then I met him through
a microecon professor, who just,

00:22:49.802 --> 00:22:51.900
he was talking about airlines.

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:53.800
And I was like, hey,
I want to talk more about airlines.

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:56.200
He was like,
you should meet this professor.

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:00.377
And I did, and then he introduced
me to several executives

00:23:00.377 --> 00:23:04.136
at the airline that he was
a board of because of that.

00:23:04.136 --> 00:23:06.745
And some of them still,
I'm in contact with and

00:23:06.745 --> 00:23:09.570
have remained advisors to my company.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.571
That's one specific-
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:23:11.571 --> 00:23:12.943
&gt;&gt; Way that GSB helped, mm-hm.

00:23:12.943 --> 00:23:13.794
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:23:13.794 --> 00:23:14.599
Thank you so much.

00:23:14.599 --> 00:23:19.520
I saw some questions out there
both from and Michael, and

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:25.080
a few other questions related to clubs and
extracurricular activities.

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:29.900
So I'd love to maybe start with Sunny.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:35.017
If you could share what
extracurriculars were you involved in?

00:23:35.017 --> 00:23:40.836
And I know a challenge of many students
is just that there are so many.

00:23:40.836 --> 00:23:45.830
But perhaps not only which ones were you
involved in, but what were the ones that

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:49.980
were most meaningful to you
during your time at the GSB?

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:51.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:54.909
I think you opened it up appropriately,
and said probably the most most

00:23:54.909 --> 00:23:58.484
challenging thing about the GSB is
that there are so many opportunities.

00:23:58.484 --> 00:24:02.170
That it does feel like you're drinking
from the firehouse until everyone kind of

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:04.612
figures out what their
appropriate study state is.

00:24:04.612 --> 00:24:08.800
For me, I chose couple of specific
activities that Were helpful.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:13.290
One was just choosing to be in a couple of
industry groups that I thought would give

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:15.720
me, again,
that broad exposure I was looking for.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:20.160
So, ended up doing way too many to begin
with, but ended up whittling it down to

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:23.630
a list that I felt was directly
relevant to what I would want to do,

00:24:23.630 --> 00:24:27.790
which included the tech, the healthcare,
and the Venture Capital Group.

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:31.950
I also mentioned earlier
that I led a trip to Greece,

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:34.350
that was probably one of the more intense,
but

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:39.580
one of the most rewarding experiences I
had, in that I got to take 20 of my fellow

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:44.660
GSB classmates to Greece to understand
the healthcare ecosystem there.

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:46.610
And then I also got involved
in student government.

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:50.890
I was the Social Chair of
the Student Committee and

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:55.800
was able to give back a little bit
to the community by using Stanford

00:24:55.800 --> 00:25:01.440
sponsored funds to throw events,
parties, activities that

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:06.130
really were able to meet
the entire needs of the community.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:10.318
Everything from your big parties and
dances but also events for children,

00:25:10.318 --> 00:25:14.321
families with children, such as
a Halloween trick or treating event.

00:25:14.321 --> 00:25:18.511
So it was a great way to get exposed to a
very broad set of the community that I may

00:25:18.511 --> 00:25:22.724
have not otherwise interacted with if
I just pursued my academic interests.

00:25:22.724 --> 00:25:24.300
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.440
I'd love to hear more
about extracurriculars,

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:29.230
just because there were so
many questions out there about them.

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:33.192
So, Marlo, can you also share perhaps
what was one of your more meaningful

00:25:33.192 --> 00:25:35.469
extracurricular activities?

00:25:35.469 --> 00:25:37.940
&gt;&gt; Sure, there's a couple I think of.

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:41.616
I know I mentioned one, which is that I
was involved in a couple investing clubs.

00:25:41.616 --> 00:25:46.272
Outside of that, I also actually helped
form a new club with a couple friends who

00:25:46.272 --> 00:25:49.418
I met first year,
which is a really cool experience.

00:25:49.418 --> 00:25:54.087
So we started a Stanford chapter of what
was called the Adam Smith Society, which

00:25:54.087 --> 00:25:58.689
is kind of a free market-oriented club
that had some chapters in other areas and

00:25:58.689 --> 00:26:00.630
it was a really cool experience.

00:26:00.630 --> 00:26:02.908
We got a lot of support from
the school to get that started.

00:26:02.908 --> 00:26:06.900
We actually, our, I think,
second year won an award for

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:09.365
kind of best new chapter,
which is exciting.

00:26:09.365 --> 00:26:11.930
We had a decent cohort of
people who are involved.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:13.066
We had speakers come through and whatnot.

00:26:13.066 --> 00:26:17.259
That was a very cool experience to see
something kind of net new brought to

00:26:17.259 --> 00:26:18.026
the school.

00:26:18.026 --> 00:26:20.140
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:23.620
There are a couple panelists,
Hana and Saran,

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:29.365
who have specifically questions to Mariana
that we want to follow back up on about,

00:26:29.365 --> 00:26:32.680
just pivoting into tech and
product management.

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:37.637
So I'd like to know from you just what
courses really helped you hone in

00:26:37.637 --> 00:26:40.170
on product management, perhaps.

00:26:40.170 --> 00:26:44.423
And then also you mentioned you had
started your own company afterwards.

00:26:44.423 --> 00:26:49.161
So, similar to Nora's,
the question of just what helped you,

00:26:49.161 --> 00:26:52.160
from the GSB, kind of go down that path?

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:55.280
How were you supported,
whether it was classes, faculty,

00:26:55.280 --> 00:27:00.130
classmates, just your experience
in terms of pivoting careers, but

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:02.290
also specifically into tech and
product management?

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:07.540
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
becoming a founder came first and

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:11.090
I sort of figured out that,
within all the things you can do in tech,

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:15.420
products was interesting for
me through that experience.

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:18.840
So becoming a founder,
it sort of came through a few things.

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:23.020
So one is that e-business class, just
like learning about the survey of tech

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:26.952
companies and how to sort of
splice through the ecosystem.

00:27:26.952 --> 00:27:31.770
Two is that internship I mentioned and
then from there sort of came in a flurry.

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:36.660
One of the most important classes
was Lean Launchpad with Steve Blank.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:40.230
And so, in that class,
it's across a few schools.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:44.060
So you pair up with folks who might
come from the engineering school or

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:45.840
other graduate programs.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.210
And your job is to build a business.

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:50.760
And so through the course of the class,

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:55.920
you come up with an idea with your team
and then you just pivoting constantly.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:28:00.220
So like every two to three weeks,
you're iterating on your idea.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:03.720
You're in the field talking
to customers constantly.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:07.590
And that class is really eye opening
because you learn how to be scrappy,

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:11.400
like one of Steve Blank's points was that
if you never get kicked out of a store,

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:12.747
you really aren't trying hard enough.

00:28:12.747 --> 00:28:17.140
[LAUGH] Or it's like, you just need
to do these these very scrappy,

00:28:17.140 --> 00:28:21.040
crazy things or to figure out how
to get things off the ground.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:27.260
And so that helped to sort of
catapult me into becoming a founder.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:31.890
At the time, we had a program where,
for a few months after graduating,

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:35.445
you could actually have
an office on campus.

00:28:35.445 --> 00:28:40.225
So I actually worked out of an office
at the GSB for a while, which is great.

00:28:40.225 --> 00:28:46.045
And then from there I ran my company and
in the sort of flow of what I was doing.

00:28:46.045 --> 00:28:49.429
I was doing all the roles,
helping with design, helping with EnG,

00:28:49.429 --> 00:28:53.830
because I had the EnG background,
helping with products, helping the sales.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:57.460
I figured out that I liked product
the most, just what gave me energy.

00:28:57.460 --> 00:29:03.420
And so when I decided to switch into
Facebook, I pursued product and marketing.

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:07.310
And thank goodness for me, because I don't
think I'd be the greatest marketer in

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:13.030
the world, I ended up finding
a great fit with product.

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:18.000
But I think we now have more
coursework dedicated to product.

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:20.730
It's a really exciting space.

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:24.980
It's a very sort of made up career,
like it didn't exist the way it is now,

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:26.310
20 years ago.

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:30.890
And so it's one where you
having a lot of flexibility and

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:33.520
the ability to really think
on your feet goes a long way,

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:36.820
which is a lot of what you
learn in business school.

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:39.770
And also part of it, to be honest,
like one of those key skills that I gained

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:46.740
that's really helpful is being able to
confidently make something up on the spot.

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:51.620
[LAUGH] Because I had to project
confidence all day long and

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:56.790
like I normally know exactly into
all details what's going on, but

00:29:56.790 --> 00:30:00.828
there's some times where it's like, you're
making what I call a 49, 51 decision.

00:30:00.828 --> 00:30:05.022
There's a 49% chance this,
and a 51% chance it's that.

00:30:05.022 --> 00:30:07.207
But no matter what call you make,

00:30:07.207 --> 00:30:12.297
you need to drive your team forward in
that path, and those are the hard ones.

00:30:12.297 --> 00:30:18.040
And then so, for those 49, 51s,
being able to, on the spot,

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:25.340
explain, help the team to become competent
behind that idea, goes a long way.

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:29.750
That's something that I learned
through a series of debates and

00:30:29.750 --> 00:30:31.510
other coursework at Stanford.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:34.560
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you so much for sharing.

00:30:36.050 --> 00:30:40.330
We have a question from Marielle, and
I hope I pronounced your name correctly,

00:30:40.330 --> 00:30:47.890
just, what is the role of the GSB in
the lives of our panelists post GSB?

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:53.120
So how have you been involved afterwards
and I first, again, point out they're

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:58.690
all here as panelists, which is an awesome
way for them to be involved and give back.

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:01.432
But, because this is the western region,

00:31:01.432 --> 00:31:06.080
I'd love to have Vidya start by
answering this question and talk about,

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:10.117
perhaps, the alumni community
up in the Pacific Northwest and

00:31:10.117 --> 00:31:14.765
in the Seattle area, where you're based,
and then we'll have someone

00:31:14.765 --> 00:31:18.914
else share what it's like in
a different part of the West Coast.

00:31:18.914 --> 00:31:22.218
&gt;&gt; I actually, I might take that question
differently than how you directed me,

00:31:22.218 --> 00:31:23.529
if that's okay, Lisa [LAUGH].

00:31:23.529 --> 00:31:25.600
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; We do have an alumni presence up here.

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:30.502
There are events that happen
from time to time like talks and

00:31:30.502 --> 00:31:33.847
we have a summer event every year, etc.

00:31:33.847 --> 00:31:38.526
And I definitely take part in those but
I actually feel like the way that GSB

00:31:38.526 --> 00:31:43.359
shows up in my life now is pretty separate
from that, which is primarily like

00:31:43.359 --> 00:31:47.760
the community of my class and
kind of a few specific sub communities.

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:52.630
So there's one thing that kind of grew
organically is there's a group of

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:57.020
about ten of us from my class
who all have deep passion for

00:31:57.020 --> 00:32:00.810
International Development work and
social issues and we formed a group

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:08.180
back in 2013 or something and have been
getting together in person once a year,

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:11.615
and five of those people
live in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:32:11.615 --> 00:32:12.750
I live in the US.

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:16.750
And we still get together in person once a
year, and we have several kind of meetings

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:18.916
throughout the year where we
choose an organization to back,

00:32:18.916 --> 00:32:20.380
an area we want to learn about, etc.

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:22.780
And they're just very, very close friends.

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:26.970
So that's been an amazing community, just
in terms of shared interest, and also,

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:28.570
we help each other career wise.

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:32.210
The other thing that's been amazing
just in the last month or so

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:37.060
is all of the stuff that's going on
right now on systemic racism and

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.860
social unrest here in the US.

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:45.180
My entire class of 2012 has come together
in this massive WhatsApp group around

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:45.990
Black Lives Matter.

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:49.589
And has really been activating each other,
and finding resources, and

00:32:49.589 --> 00:32:53.129
finding actions that we can take
collectively, both with the GSB, and

00:32:53.129 --> 00:32:54.965
kind of outside in our communities.

00:32:54.965 --> 00:32:58.735
And that's actually just been phenomenal
to connect with people that way, and

00:32:58.735 --> 00:33:01.602
have so many leaders that have
a lot of power out of the world,

00:33:01.602 --> 00:33:04.487
trying to kind of activate
themselves to make a difference.

00:33:04.487 --> 00:33:08.642
So it shows up a lot, and it's the most
powerful part of going to the GSB,

00:33:08.642 --> 00:33:10.435
is your classmates, I think.

00:33:10.435 --> 00:33:13.290
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you so much for sharing.

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:19.110
Let's go back to perhaps Marlo,
who's in Los Angeles.

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:21.110
So also in kind of non Bay Area,

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:25.880
if you could tell us how the GSB
shows up in your life post GSB?

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:31.174
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, so
even from a macro perspective,

00:33:31.174 --> 00:33:35.256
similarly to what said,
my class is very close.

00:33:35.256 --> 00:33:39.050
We have all different sorts of
programming and interaction points.

00:33:39.050 --> 00:33:43.676
We, even during COVID, started doing
twice a month, sort of career talks,

00:33:43.676 --> 00:33:47.252
where people on Saturday
mornings talk about what they do.

00:33:47.252 --> 00:33:50.129
And some of the interesting things about
this, just to continue to give people

00:33:50.129 --> 00:33:53.880
interesting ideas of career paths, which I
think is really cool that just sprung up.

00:33:53.880 --> 00:33:55.140
And then more of a local front,

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:58.250
I mean, we have actually
a pretty vibrant LA community.

00:33:58.250 --> 00:33:59.460
Thinking about even tonight,

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:02.400
my class is having to get together
to celebrate one of our classmates.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:05.018
She's finishing residency today.

00:34:05.018 --> 00:34:08.646
And there's a number of us, obviously,
a small group we get together.

00:34:08.646 --> 00:34:12.830
But pretty cool that we have a tight
knit community down here that we have,

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:16.554
especially during times where
we're allowed to see each other.

00:34:16.554 --> 00:34:19.421
Birthday parties, and get togethers
,and book clubs, and stuff like that,

00:34:19.421 --> 00:34:21.690
which I think is cool,
especially removed from the Bay Area.

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:24.160
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.290
And then I'd love maybe Mariana,

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:29.455
if you could chime in as
representing the Bay Area.

00:34:29.455 --> 00:34:33.434
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Just kind of what role does the GSB

00:34:33.434 --> 00:34:35.530
play in your life as an alum?

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:38.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm lucky.

00:34:38.700 --> 00:34:39.670
I'm super close.

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.930
I think it depends on time.

00:34:41.930 --> 00:34:47.990
So the first year after, the GSBers who
were in the Bay Area hung out constantly.

00:34:50.430 --> 00:34:53.165
Yeah, I think I'd see a GSBer
probably every other day.

00:34:53.165 --> 00:34:56.560
[LAUGH] We were really,
really, really tight.

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:57.830
It's waned over time.

00:34:57.830 --> 00:35:02.440
So now I have a select group of folks who
were actually across my class in videos

00:35:02.440 --> 00:35:06.460
class who I hang out with a lot,
just my friend group.

00:35:06.460 --> 00:35:12.330
And then otherwise,
I just see people very often.

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:17.146
So coming from the Bay Area just
absolutely filled with GSBers,

00:35:17.146 --> 00:35:19.743
including the one I work with now.

00:35:19.743 --> 00:35:24.060
And so
I just normal course coming across GSBers.

00:35:24.060 --> 00:35:27.687
In terms of formal programming,
my class has,

00:35:27.687 --> 00:35:32.323
we used to do an annual football game,
so a massive cookout.

00:35:32.323 --> 00:35:37.252
And then go to the game together,
and we'd have a DJ playing.

00:35:37.252 --> 00:35:41.390
We'd have caterers come in,
it's this big deal.

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:45.190
Obviously, I don't think
that's happening this year.

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:52.039
But otherwise, honestly, in the Bay Area,
it happens pretty organically,

00:35:52.039 --> 00:35:57.666
because GSBers are everywhere,
and so that's been my experience.

00:35:57.666 --> 00:35:59.910
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:04.070
I'm going to direct this next
question to Norris as the most recent

00:36:04.070 --> 00:36:05.545
graduate of the program.

00:36:05.545 --> 00:36:10.560
Jordan in the audience asked just how did

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:16.210
classes adapt to stay relevant to
perhaps what may have been going

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:21.950
on in the world, or
in business at that time?

00:36:21.950 --> 00:36:25.070
Did you experience
anything like that where

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:29.260
there was innovation taking place in the
classroom itself by the course offerings?

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:34.430
&gt;&gt; I'm probably not going to answer
the question how you're hoping.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:39.071
So I was really curious about
what GSB was doing with COVID-19.

00:36:39.071 --> 00:36:43.604
And so I actually knew a professor who I
reached out to for some other stuff, and

00:36:43.604 --> 00:36:46.595
I asked if I can participate in class.

00:36:46.595 --> 00:36:49.795
And so he did, of course,
a lot of lectures are on Zoom, and

00:36:49.795 --> 00:36:51.725
he did his lecture on Zoom.

00:36:51.725 --> 00:36:54.395
And that was one way that
he adopted the class, and

00:36:54.395 --> 00:36:56.955
I thought it was actually
really well done.

00:36:56.955 --> 00:36:59.185
And having taken the course
both in person and

00:36:59.185 --> 00:37:05.380
online, I actually noticed very
little decrease in quality.

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:08.901
And there was still audience,
or student participation.

00:37:08.901 --> 00:37:11.612
They brought in a guest speaker as well.

00:37:11.612 --> 00:37:15.000
And then the guest speaker was
really engaging in a lot of student

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:16.260
questions as well.

00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:18.270
And they did a case review.

00:37:18.270 --> 00:37:22.840
So talk about adaptation, I mean, that was
just done on the fly in a couple weeks.

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:28.090
And that was not just one professor, but
it was en masse across the entire program.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:30.932
So that's one way.

00:37:30.932 --> 00:37:35.864
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and just to echo on that,
it's something that happened very quickly,

00:37:35.864 --> 00:37:40.123
and that's great to hear that you
were able to take part in that, and

00:37:40.123 --> 00:37:41.564
see that experience.

00:37:41.564 --> 00:37:44.638
And perhaps we could have someone
else chime in about that, and

00:37:44.638 --> 00:37:49.500
just about the courses offered during that
time in their relevancy to that time.

00:37:49.500 --> 00:37:54.270
Maybe Sunny, you could chime in if there
were any courses you saw developed,

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:58.340
or offered that were specific to
just changes happening in business,

00:37:58.340 --> 00:37:59.930
or the world at that time.

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:03.224
&gt;&gt; I think one of the major
things that I saw, and

00:38:03.224 --> 00:38:08.266
was actually a part of was expanding
the GSBs healthcare offerings.

00:38:08.266 --> 00:38:11.439
And healthcare is an industry that's
growing tremendously quickly, and

00:38:11.439 --> 00:38:12.452
it has been for a while.

00:38:12.452 --> 00:38:15.556
But especially from the business side,
the tech side,

00:38:15.556 --> 00:38:17.940
which Stanford is so good at excelling.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:23.400
They've been able to bring together
even more expertise in the area.

00:38:23.400 --> 00:38:27.890
And I think that's actually shown up
in either directly as a result of,

00:38:27.890 --> 00:38:30.340
or as part of student demand.

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:35.836
At least in my class,
I know that the healthcare club was one of

00:38:35.836 --> 00:38:41.027
the largest groups that was
active from a industry focus.

00:38:41.027 --> 00:38:44.690
And what I found was that
the GSB was very responsive, and

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:48.733
that if they get demand from
the students to explore a new area,

00:38:48.733 --> 00:38:52.804
find some faculty support,
launch programs, launch clubs.

00:38:52.804 --> 00:38:54.234
As long as you have student interest,

00:38:54.234 --> 00:38:56.212
you can almost always get
something off the ground.

00:38:56.212 --> 00:39:00.430
And I absolutely saw it happen in real
time with healthcare offerings at the GSB.

00:39:01.700 --> 00:39:02.840
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thank you.

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:10.288
I see a few questions that relate
to just kind of the general

00:39:10.288 --> 00:39:16.180
the GSB, and
this is a really broad question.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:22.290
We only have a few minutes left,
but I would love to pose that out.

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:26.656
We'll start maybe with video just
about kind of not just why the GSB,

00:39:26.656 --> 00:39:28.620
because you've touched on
that a little bit already.

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:31.670
But also,
how did you know it was the right time for

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:33.240
you to apply to business law?

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:35.940
We're seeing a couple few
questions out there around that.

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:40.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think there's
probably as many answers to this

00:39:40.190 --> 00:39:44.700
question as people that you talk to,
because it's such a personal decision.

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:48.897
In terms of timing, I think for me,
again, like I mentioned before,

00:39:48.897 --> 00:39:52.242
I kind of knew I wanted to gain
a certain skill set that was

00:39:52.242 --> 00:39:54.900
around understanding how companies work.

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:58.347
And understanding how you can well
run an organization, and then go and

00:39:58.347 --> 00:40:01.060
apply that in the field of
international development.

00:40:01.060 --> 00:40:05.490
And I felt I had accumulated a certain
number of years in experiences

00:40:06.760 --> 00:40:09.608
out there in the private sector,
and I was kind of ready.

00:40:09.608 --> 00:40:13.080
I just kind of felt like it was like okay,
the marginal return of another year, or

00:40:13.080 --> 00:40:16.560
another couple years, versus going and
actually doing what I want to do.

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:20.100
Isn't worth it and that so I was I think
four years out when I went to the GSB.

00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:21.560
So that that motivated the timing,

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:24.850
it was gut feel, I mean who knows
if it was the right decision.

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:30.530
And then in terms of why Stanford,
for me, there was one personal factor,

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:33.630
which is my now husband was
getting his PhD at Stanford.

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:37.100
So that was an attractive feature
being back in the same place.

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:39.310
But that actually wasn't
the primary motivator,

00:40:39.310 --> 00:40:42.080
I think I could have
easily like moved out and

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:45.390
gotten a job in the Bay Area if we
just wanted to be in the same place.

00:40:45.390 --> 00:40:49.220
I think for me, it was really
the combination of things that

00:40:49.220 --> 00:40:52.340
Stanford was kind of really
exceptionally known for.

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:56.788
So one was the interdisciplinary nature of
all the schools, knowing I wanted to work

00:40:56.788 --> 00:41:00.750
kind of in healthcare and
innovation that was usually attractive.

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:02.866
And really, I think was really true,

00:41:02.866 --> 00:41:06.252
like what everyone says is even
more true once you get there.

00:41:06.252 --> 00:41:10.808
I think a lot of focus really on personal
growth and kind of developing as a leader,

00:41:10.808 --> 00:41:15.297
which I didn't get that vibe necessarily
from other schools that I looked at and

00:41:15.297 --> 00:41:17.709
it is the most important
thing to invest and

00:41:17.709 --> 00:41:21.950
I think in ourselves at that time today
before we kind of go on to other roles.

00:41:23.680 --> 00:41:27.095
Unless there's just kind of like the
innovation spirit I think that's part of

00:41:27.095 --> 00:41:30.419
Stanford which now is like so well known
and was well known at that time too.

00:41:30.419 --> 00:41:34.936
But I think there's just something really
special there about how you want the world

00:41:34.936 --> 00:41:37.099
to be, you have the ability to create and

00:41:37.099 --> 00:41:41.166
that is like just such a cool thing to
be around even if it's people that are,

00:41:41.166 --> 00:41:45.460
I'm not in tech, but it's cool to be
around people that are doing that in tech.

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:48.748
So it's like a combination of all those
things that felt really special and

00:41:48.748 --> 00:41:51.318
talking to folks that had been
there that validated that.

00:41:51.318 --> 00:41:53.616
&gt;&gt; Yeah, awesome, thanks, Vidia.

00:41:53.616 --> 00:41:55.700
So you guys, we have three minutes left.

00:41:55.700 --> 00:42:00.020
I do want to wrap up with one question
that I'll direct towards two of you.

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:04.200
Which is,
we've had a lot of folks out there ask

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:09.130
questions around just admissions advice,
and just general things like that.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:13.332
And we do have information sessions
that focus a bit more on the admissions

00:42:13.332 --> 00:42:17.735
process, which I'll talk about in
a minute, but I would love maybe Sunny and

00:42:17.735 --> 00:42:20.274
then Marlowe, so we'll start with Sunny.

00:42:20.274 --> 00:42:24.198
If you could go back to your pre-GSB self,

00:42:24.198 --> 00:42:28.649
maybe what is a piece of
advice you might give?

00:42:28.649 --> 00:42:29.765
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:42:29.765 --> 00:42:33.147
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, [LAUGH] I think that probably

00:42:33.147 --> 00:42:38.223
the most important thing that
I would give to myself prior

00:42:38.223 --> 00:42:43.610
to coming to the GSB is
absolutely keep an open mind.

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:47.960
Be willing to take on a broad exposure but
also don't try and do everything.

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:51.780
I think the number one thing that at least
I ran into I know many of my colleagues

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:55.000
did, was there's just so
much opportunity that being able

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:59.480
to narrow it down to what you're
passionate about, is not easy.

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:03.100
It's almost like it's a curse of
having too many good options, and

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:05.470
too many good opportunities
to create pursuit.

00:43:05.470 --> 00:43:10.660
So while you should absolutely take the
opportunity to try many different things,

00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:12.360
figure out what really calls to you and

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:15.300
then spend as much time
as you can pursuing that.

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.589
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.
And Marlowe,

00:43:18.589 --> 00:43:24.545
anything kind of advice you
would give to your pre-GSB self?

00:43:24.545 --> 00:43:27.831
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Sunny took mine which has made
the most of the time because there's

00:43:27.831 --> 00:43:29.530
always so many things to choose from.

00:43:29.530 --> 00:43:34.420
But I guess the other one I would say
too is, I just long lines of being

00:43:34.420 --> 00:43:38.330
